"""Corrections to historical documentation claims, never to implementation."""
import re

def correct(text):
    paragraphs={
    'Just over half a pixel.':'The 0.6-pixel tolerance is a project simplification setting, not a proven boundary between sampling noise and intentional writing. Nearest rounding can move a point by up to half a pixel on each axis, or about 0.707 pixels in Euclidean distance; error relative to a line reconstructed from rounded endpoints is a different quantity again. Raising epsilon generally removes more vertices and may erase real small features; lowering it retains more detail and more sampling noise. No literature result or reviewed sweep here establishes 0.6 as optimal.',
    'A threshold of 75 degrees permits':'A threshold of 75 degrees permits substantial direction change at a skeleton junction. The qualitative trade-off is between leaving a plausible continuation split and joining branches that were separate. Incorrect joining can alter stroke order, connections and shape, not merely the stroke count. The exact 75-degree value is a project heuristic; its optimality and asymmetric error costs are not established by the algorithm citation.',
    'The band is nominally 0 to 1, so it might seem':'The band is nominally 0 to 1, but its measured boundaries are uncertain. Margins can tolerate that uncertainty. The code uses different admissible extents for x-height, ascender and descender classes; these are heuristic gates rather than confidence intervals inferred from a measured error distribution.',
    'The asymmetry between 1.42 and 1.25':'The 1.42 and 1.25 upper-band tests serve different classes: rejecting excess height in an x-height letter versus demanding some extension for an ascender. Their overlap means a height between these values may be admissible under either class test. It does not prove a measured ambiguity interval or establish that contamination always exceeds a particular height. No reviewed source establishes these exact cutoffs as universal.',
    'The spacing used throughout layout.':'The 0.35 mm sampling step controls trajectory point density. Finer spacing increases point storage and later processing; coarser spacing gives fewer samples and can underrepresent curved geometry. Segment-based drawing and motion interpolation also matter, so uniform samples alone do not guarantee continuous raster coverage, smoother physical motion or constant actual pen speed. This exact step is a project setting, not a literature optimum.',
    'The alternation of aligning to an estimate':'Aligning variants to an estimate and then recomputing that estimate is iterative refinement. It can reduce misregistration, but this implementation does not establish a monotone decrease for every quality measure or a global optimum. Two rounds are the implementation budget, not a demonstrated sufficient number for every writer or variant pool.',
    'This is a convolution along the stroke with a three-point kernel':'This is convolution along the sampled path with a three-point kernel. Unit-sum weights preserve constant coordinate sequences and make the operation equivariant to translating the whole path. They do not preserve every curve\'s size: averaging can round corners and shrink curved structures even when the weights sum to one.',
    'Two passes are applied. Applying the same small kernel':'Two passes are applied. Away from endpoint handling, repeated convolution is equivalent to convolution with the combined kernel. Additional passes smooth more strongly and can also remove genuine small features. The exact pass count is not supported here by an independent optimum or a universal curvature-preservation bound.',
    'Both trained networks accept one exact input size and nothing else':'The current pipeline prepares crops on the trained fixed canvas. Convolution, adaptive pooling and recurrence do not by themselves require every source image to have identical dimensions, and this implementation does not justify the canvas by an image-flattened final layer. The fixed preparation instead supplies a consistent input distribution and sequence budget for these saved models.',
    'It is the one tuned constant, and the reasoning behind it':'This fraction thresholds the row-ink profile to estimate the letter-body band. A clear gap between body and ascender densities can make the result insensitive within an interval, but sparse text, capitals and unusual writing can remove that gap. Lower values admit more rows; higher values can collapse the band. Its exact value is a project heuristic, not an independently established measurement-error threshold.',
    'Slant is the largest single source of variation':'Slant is one source of writer and page variation. Shear augmentation exposes training to changed stroke inclination, but it also changes horizontal geometry and interpolation. It is not established here as the largest source of variation.',
    'A fixed distance threshold would be wrong per writer':'A percentile gate ranks variants within a writer instead of imposing one absolute distance cutoff across all writers. This can tolerate a writer whose whole pool differs from the cross-writer reference, but also retains poor samples if every variant is poor. Tie handling, small-pool exceptions and earlier filters mean a stated retained fraction is not necessarily the fraction of all original glyphs kept.',
    'The ownership problem is where the segmenter is least principled':'The initial ownership follows the seam bands; subsequent corrections are project-specific rules for candidate fragments and connections. Separate helpers make those conditions inspectable. Historical ablation claims require the corresponding run records; the existence of a helper does not establish how its contribution was measured.',
    }
    for start,replacement in paragraphs.items():
        text=re.sub(r'(?m)^'+re.escape(start)+r'[^\n]*(?:\n(?!\s*$)[^\n]*)*',lambda m:replacement,text)
    text=text.replace('and it is correct for the same reason','but this greedy order does not prove globally correct reconstruction')
    text=text.replace('Neither can be expressed as the other.','The two interfaces serve different callers; count and spacing can be related through total path length, subject to endpoint and rounding conventions.')
    text=text.replace('which spans the range of real handwriting, from upright to the steepest cursive in this project\'s writers','which specifies the sampled augmentation range; it is not a measured bound on all real writers')
    text=text.replace('weights, which sum to 1 so the stroke is not scaled or shifted','weights, which sum to 1 and preserve a constant coordinate signal; curved geometry can still shrink')
    text=text.replace('would slow the segmenter measurably on every page','adds work; no per-page timing effect is established here')
    text=text.replace('the code has no dependencies','the numerical routines expose their calculations explicitly')
    text=text.replace("draws by stepping along the path, so a sparse region is drawn from fewer samples; with a long enough gap the round pen's footprints no longer overlap and the stroke appears as a row of dots", "uses distances to complete line segments in DrawPolyline, so sparse vertices do not inherently create dotted straight segments; resampling changes the represented geometry and amount of segment work")
    text=text.replace("emits a move per point, so the machine's motion is smoother where points are dense. Even spacing gives even pen speed, which is visible in the drawn line", "emits moves between points; point density affects command count and curve approximation. Actual speed also depends on pulse timing and mechanics, so even spacing does not establish constant physical pen speed")
    return text
